(function () {
  const expanded = {
    "1|The Great Conversation": [
      "<strong>How to picture the idea:</strong> Imagine writers across time answering, revising, challenging, and borrowing from earlier books, while later readers change what those books mean. A Roman poet can rework a Greek epic; a modern novelist can question older values; a new reader can notice voices earlier audiences overlooked.",
      "There is no single meeting or final list of participants. The metaphor describes an ongoing exchange, not proof that every culture shares one origin or that one canon is permanent. Ask who has been included, who has been left out, and how a later work responds to an earlier one."
    ],
    "1|Cinderella": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> In a familiar European version, Cinderella is made to do exhausting housework by her stepmother and stepsisters. When a royal ball is announced, they go dressed for court and leave her behind. A magical helper gives her fine clothes and a coach, warning that the magic will end at midnight. The prince is taken with her, but she escapes before he learns who she is.",
      "<strong>Ending and variants:</strong> She loses a shoe on the stairs. The prince searches the kingdom, and the shoe fits Cinderella, revealing her identity and leading to marriage and a new life. Helpers, punishments, and endings differ across Cinderella tales. Treat this as a family of folktales, not one fixed original, and compare what each version says about work, kindness, marriage, and justice."
    ],
    "2|The Great Gatsby": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Nick Carraway rents a modest house on Long Island beside the mansion of Jay Gatsby, a mysterious millionaire whose parties attract crowds but few real friends. Nick learns that Gatsby and Nick’s cousin Daisy Buchanan loved one another before the war. Gatsby has built his fortune and home partly in the hope that Daisy will return. Nick arranges a reunion, and the old romance briefly comes alive.",
      "<strong>Turning point and ending:</strong> Gatsby wants Daisy to leave her husband Tom and restore the past. During a tense trip to New York, Tom exposes Gatsby’s criminal business, and Daisy—driving Gatsby’s car—strikes and kills Tom’s mistress Myrtle Wilson. Gatsby accepts blame to protect Daisy. Myrtle’s husband George is led to believe Gatsby was responsible; he shoots Gatsby and then himself. Daisy and Tom retreat into their wealth, and almost no one attends Gatsby’s funeral. Nick leaves disillusioned by privilege, fantasy, and carelessness."
    ],
    "2|The Catcher in the Rye": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Holden Caulfield narrates from a place where he is receiving care. Just expelled from Pencey Prep, he leaves early and spends several days alone in New York City instead of immediately facing his parents. He wanders hotels, bars, museums, and streets, meeting people but repeatedly feeling disappointed by what he calls adult phoniness. His funny, defensive voice is shaped by grief and loneliness.",
      "<strong>Ending and change:</strong> Holden thinks about his younger brother Allie, who has died, and looks for connection with his sister Phoebe. He considers running away, but Phoebe insists on joining him. At the zoo, he watches her ride the carousel in the rain and feels a rare happiness as she reaches for the gold ring. He decides not to leave. The book ends with a small movement toward accepting change and the people he misses, not a claim that he is fully recovered."
    ],
    "3|The Republic": [
      "<strong>Argument:</strong> Plato presents a conversation led by Socrates, who asks what justice is. After several definitions fail, the speakers imagine a city so they can examine justice on a larger scale. They debate education, the duties of rulers and guardians, the danger of appetite and power, and the idea that a just person’s inner life has order rather than being ruled by selfish desire.",
      "<strong>Key image and conclusion:</strong> In the Allegory of the Cave, prisoners mistake shadows for the whole world. A difficult education turns one prisoner toward the light, and returning to explain the truth is painful. Education becomes a reorientation of the mind, not merely filling it with facts. The dialogue argues for rule guided by knowledge, while leaving readers to debate the political costs of Plato’s ideal city."
    ],
    "3|Poetics": [
      "<strong>Argument:</strong> Aristotle examines how poetry and drama represent human action. His surviving discussion gives special attention to tragedy: characters act, choices produce consequences, and a plot arranges those actions into a meaningful whole. A strong plot is more than a chain of surprises; events connect by probability or necessity so that a change in fortune feels earned.",
      "<strong>What to remember:</strong> Reversal changes the direction of events, recognition changes what a character knows, and suffering makes consequences visible. Aristotle connects tragic experience with pity, fear, and catharsis, though scholars debate the exact meaning of catharsis. These are tools for analyzing how a play works on its audience, not a recipe every tragedy must follow."
    ],
    "3|Oedipus Rex": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> A plague devastates Thebes. King Oedipus promises to find who murdered the former king, Laius, because the city has been told that the killer must be found. The prophet Tiresias resists the investigation, then says Oedipus himself is responsible. Oedipus suspects a political plot. His wife Jocasta tries to calm him by describing the prophecy she and Laius once tried to defeat.",
      "<strong>Recognition and ending:</strong> A messenger and an old shepherd reveal that Oedipus was taken from his birth parents as a baby. On the road years earlier, he unknowingly killed Laius, his father, and later married Jocasta, his mother. Jocasta kills herself; Oedipus blinds himself and asks to be exiled. An investigation meant to cleanse the city becomes a devastating discovery about identity, knowledge, and responsibility."
    ],
    "3|Antigone": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> After brothers Eteocles and Polyneices kill one another in civil war, their uncle Creon becomes king. He honors Eteocles but orders that Polyneices remain unburied as punishment for attacking Thebes. Antigone believes burial is a sacred duty owed to her brother, so she secretly scatters dust over his body. Creon arrests her even after she admits what she did; her sister Ismene is too late to share the blame.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Creon’s son Haemon, engaged to Antigone, pleads for mercy. The prophet Tiresias warns that the gods reject Creon’s decision. Creon changes his mind, but reaches the tomb after Antigone has hanged herself. Haemon kills himself beside her, and Creon’s wife Eurydice dies after hearing the news. Creon survives, grieving the family he destroyed. The play asks what happens when political authority refuses to listen to family, religious duty, and counsel."
    ],
    "3|Aeneid": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> After Troy is destroyed, Aeneas escapes carrying his father Anchises and guiding his son Ascanius, while survivors follow in search of a new home. Storms and divine hostility delay them. In Carthage, Queen Dido and Aeneas fall in love, and he briefly imagines staying. The god Mercury reminds him that his people’s future lies in Italy. Aeneas sails away; Dido, abandoned, kills herself and curses the future conflict between their peoples.",
      "<strong>Ending and purpose:</strong> In Italy, Aeneas must fight Turnus and local forces before the Trojans can settle. The war costs many lives, including his young ally Pallas. The epic ends when Aeneas defeats Turnus in single combat; seeing Pallas’s belt on his enemy, he kills Turnus in anger. Rome has not yet been founded within the story, but the poem links Aeneas’s painful journey to its legendary origins and asks what a public destiny costs individuals."
    ],
    "3|Meditations": [
      "<strong>What is in the book:</strong> This is not a continuous story but private notes Marcus Aurelius wrote while serving as Roman emperor. He reminds himself to meet annoyance without cruelty, act fairly, notice how brief life is, and avoid being controlled by praise, anger, or fear. He often imagines that other people act from limited understanding rather than from a plan to harm him.",
      "<strong>How to read it:</strong> This advice is practice, not a claim that difficult feelings disappear. Stoic discipline separates what depends on one’s choices—judgment, intention, conduct—from what does not—other people’s opinions, illness, or death. The repetitions make the book feel like a person returning to the same ethical work each day."
    ],
    "4|Epic of Gilgamesh": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Gilgamesh, king of Uruk, is powerful but abuses his people. The gods create Enkidu, a wild man, and send him to challenge the king. They fight, then become close companions. Together they defeat the guardian Humbaba and later kill the Bull of Heaven. The goddess Ishtar demands punishment, and Enkidu dies. Grief turns Gilgamesh’s strength into terror: if Enkidu can die, so can he.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Gilgamesh travels to find Utnapishtim, the survivor of an ancient flood who was granted eternal life. Utnapishtim challenges him to stay awake, and Gilgamesh fails. He briefly obtains a plant said to restore youth, but a snake steals it while he bathes. He returns to Uruk without immortality. The city’s walls and the memory of his deeds suggest a human legacy that outlasts an individual, though the poem does not pretend death is easily conquered."
    ],
    "4|Genesis: Noah and the Flood": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> In Genesis, God sees widespread violence and corruption and decides to flood the earth. Noah is instructed to build an ark for his family and representatives of the animals. The rain and rising waters cover the land; the ark preserves life until the flood recedes. Noah sends birds out to learn whether dry ground has returned. A dove eventually brings back an olive leaf, and the family leaves the ark.",
      "<strong>Ending and context:</strong> Noah makes an altar, and God establishes a covenant not to destroy all life by flood again; the rainbow becomes its sign. Animal counts and other details vary across parts of the biblical text and later retellings, so keep the assigned version in view. The narrative moves from destruction toward preservation and a renewed obligation to protect life."
    ],
    "4|Malakas and Maganda": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> In a well-known Philippine origin-story version, a bird flies over a world of sea and sky and hears a bamboo stalk being struck. It pecks the bamboo open, and a man and woman emerge: Malakas and Maganda, names often glossed as strong and beautiful. Their descendants populate the land, connecting human beginnings to a familiar plant, a bird, and the Philippine landscape.",
      "<strong>Why versions matter:</strong> There is no single written master copy that fixes every detail for all Philippine communities. Retellings differ in the bird, bamboo, first couple, and origin of the world around them. Learn the version your class assigned, and describe this as one traditional origin narrative rather than a universal account for every Filipino people."
    ],
    "4|The Odyssey": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> After the Trojan War, Odysseus tries to sail home to Ithaca, but the journey lasts ten years. He tells of the Cyclops Polyphemus, whom he blinds before escaping; the enchantress Circe; the dead he consults in the underworld; the Sirens; and the monsters Scylla and Charybdis. His men are lost after disobeying warnings about the sun god’s cattle. Odysseus reaches Ithaca alone, while his wife Penelope and son Telemachus face suitors consuming the household’s wealth and demanding that Penelope marry.",
      "<strong>Return and ending:</strong> Athena disguises Odysseus as a beggar so he can test who remains loyal. Telemachus recognizes his father, and Penelope announces a bow contest that only Odysseus can win. He reveals himself, defeats the suitors with Telemachus and servants, and reunites with Penelope after she tests him with a secret about their bed. The poem celebrates homecoming but also shows how violently Odysseus restores control."
    ],
    "4|The Hunger Games": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> In the future nation of Panem, the Capitol forces each district to send one girl and one boy to a televised fight in which only one tribute is meant to survive. When twelve-year-old Primrose Everdeen is selected, her older sister Katniss volunteers in her place. Katniss is sent from poor District 12 with Peeta Mellark. Training, sponsors, alliances, and the arena turn their survival into entertainment for the Capitol.",
      "<strong>Turning point and ending:</strong> Katniss befriends Rue, a young tribute from another district, but Rue is killed. When the rules appear to demand that either Katniss or Peeta kill the other, they threaten to eat poisonous berries together. The Capitol retreats and declares them co-victors. Their choice saves them but publicly humiliates the government, making Katniss a symbol whose political consequences neither teenager fully controls."
    ],
    "5|Everyman": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> In this medieval morality play, God sees people absorbed in worldly life and sends Death to summon Everyman for an account of his life. Everyman asks for time and searches for a companion. Fellowship, Kindred, Cousin, and Goods promise support at first, then abandon him when the journey becomes serious. He turns to Confession and receives penance; Good Deeds, initially too weak to travel, grows stronger as he prepares.",
      "<strong>Ending and lesson:</strong> Beauty, Strength, Discretion, and the Five Wits accompany Everyman for part of the journey, then leave him as he approaches the grave. Good Deeds remains. The characters are personifications, so the plot is an allegory about death and judgment rather than realistic psychology. The play teaches its Christian audience that moral action—not wealth, social ties, or physical beauty—is what accompanies a person beyond death."
    ],
    "5|Le Morte d’Arthur": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Malory gathers many Arthurian adventures into one cycle. Merlin helps the young Arthur claim kingship by drawing a sword from a stone, and Arthur builds a court around the Round Table. Knights travel on quests, fight rivals, rescue people, and seek the Holy Grail. Lancelot becomes Arthur’s greatest knight, but his love for Queen Guinevere conflicts with loyalty to the king. The Grail quest reveals that courage alone is not enough: spiritual purity matters in many versions.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Lancelot and Guinevere’s relationship fractures the fellowship. Political enemies exploit the scandal, and Arthur’s son Mordred rises against him while Arthur is away. At the battle of Camlann, Arthur and Mordred wound one another mortally. Arthur is carried away toward Avalon, leaving uncertainty about whether he is dead or will return. The kingdom’s fall turns the promise of chivalric unity into a story about betrayal, divided loyalties, and the limits of heroic rule."
    ],
    "5|Beowulf": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> The Danish king Hrothgar’s hall, Heorot, is attacked at night by Grendel. Beowulf, a young warrior from the Geats, sails to help and fights the monster without a weapon, tearing off its arm. Grendel’s mother then attacks to avenge her son. Beowulf follows her to an underwater lair and kills her with an ancient sword. He returns home with fame and later becomes king.",
      "<strong>Final battle:</strong> After ruling for many years, Beowulf faces a dragon awakened when someone steals a cup from its hoard. His warriors flee; only the young Wiglaf stays to help. Together they kill the dragon, but Beowulf is mortally wounded. He asks to see the treasure and appoints Wiglaf before dying. The Geats build a funeral mound and fear their neighbors will attack. The poem honors courage while showing that even the greatest hero cannot stop time or secure the future forever. <a href='https://assets.cambridge.org/97805211/00625/excerpt/9780521100625_excerpt.pdf' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Cambridge University Press introduction ↗</a>"
    ],
    "5|Inferno": [
      "<strong>Journey:</strong> At midlife, the pilgrim Dante finds himself lost in a dark wood and blocked by beasts. The Roman poet Virgil leads him through Hell, a vast descending landscape divided into circles. Dante meets people whose choices in life determine their punishment: the lustful are swept by a storm, fortune-tellers walk with their heads turned backward, and traitors are frozen in ice. The punishments mirror the sins in a principle often called contrapasso.",
      "<strong>Ending and purpose:</strong> Dante hears political enemies and famous figures tell their stories, and learns that pity is not the same as understanding justice. At the bottom, Satan is trapped in ice, chewing on three great traitors. Virgil and Dante climb down his body, pass through the earth’s center, and emerge to see the stars before the next part of the journey. The poem combines spiritual allegory with the politics of Dante’s Italy. <a href='https://richmond.la.utexas.edu/wp-content/uploads/2015/09/9_Sept-28-Dante-1010-1039.pdf' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>University of Texas at Austin guide to Dante ↗</a>"
    ],
    "5|Arthurian Grail romances": [
      "<strong>Story world:</strong> These are related medieval romances, not one fixed novel. Knights of Arthur’s court hear of the Grail and set out separately. Each journey tests a different quality: courage in battle, loyalty to others, humility, restraint, and readiness to recognize spiritual meaning. Lancelot’s skill cannot erase his conflict between love for Guinevere and his vows; Gawain’s pride or impulsiveness can distort his judgment; Perceval must learn to ask the right questions.",
      "<strong>Endings vary:</strong> In later Christianized versions, Galahad is pure enough to see the Grail fully, while other knights fail or receive only partial vision. Chrétien de Troyes’s unfinished Perceval differs from later Lancelot-Grail cycles and Malory’s version. Identify the author and ending of the assigned account instead of combining every Grail story into one plot."
    ],
    "7|Hamlet": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Prince Hamlet grieves his father when the dead king’s ghost says that Claudius, Hamlet’s uncle, murdered him and married his widow Gertrude. Hamlet is unsure whether to trust the ghost, so he stages a play that reenacts the alleged murder and watches Claudius react. He delays, argues with himself, and mistakenly kills Polonius while spying through a curtain. Ophelia, Polonius’s daughter and Hamlet’s former love, loses her father and later dies; her brother Laertes returns seeking revenge.",
      "<strong>Ending and the famous line:</strong> Claudius arranges for Hamlet to be killed in a rigged fencing match. Poisoned swords and a poisoned drink set off a chain of deaths: Gertrude, Laertes, Claudius, and Hamlet all die. Fortinbras arrives to claim Denmark. Earlier, in Act 3, Scene 1, “To be or not to be” weighs enduring life’s pain against ending it, while fearing what may follow death. It is a reflection on suffering and the unknown, not simply a decision about whether to kill Claudius. <a href='https://shakespeare.folger.edu/downloads/pdf/hamlet_PDF_FolgerShakespeare.pdf' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Folger Shakespeare Library synopsis and text ↗</a>"
    ],
    "7|A Modest Proposal": [
      "<strong>Argument disguised as a plan:</strong> Swift’s speaker describes Irish poverty in the language of a confident policy adviser. He proposes that poor families sell one-year-old children as food to wealthy landlords, then calculates the supposed financial benefits. He presents the plan as sensible arithmetic, even claiming it would reduce poverty. That calm tone is part of the shock.",
      "<strong>What the satire attacks:</strong> The speaker’s willingness to price children exposes a society that treats poor Irish people as a burden or resource rather than as human beings. Swift targets English exploitation, absentee landlords, and policy makers’ indifference. The proposal is not sincere. Its grotesque conclusion makes readers confront how dehumanizing economic language can become when it ignores people’s lives."
    ],
    "7|Candide": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Candide grows up in a sheltered castle and is taught by Pangloss that their world is the best possible one. After falling in love with Cunégonde, he is expelled and forced through war, an earthquake, religious persecution, and exploitation. He finds Pangloss again, meets survivors thought dead, and travels from Europe to South America. In Eldorado, he discovers a peaceful, wealthy society, but leaves with jewels because he still hopes to find Cunégonde.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Candide and his companions reunite after many losses, but money and romance do not make their troubles disappear. A neighbor’s modest farm offers another model: work, care for one another, and attend to what is directly in front of you. Candide rejects Pangloss’s endless explanations with the decision to cultivate their garden. The ending favors practical responsibility over abstract optimism without claiming that suffering has a neat explanation."
    ],
    "7|Rights of Man": [
      "<strong>Argument:</strong> Paine writes in defense of the French Revolution and against Edmund Burke’s defense of inherited political authority. He argues that people possess natural rights and that governments exist to protect those rights, not to pass power automatically from one generation to the next. A constitution should express the will of living citizens and make representatives answerable to them.",
      "<strong>Why it mattered:</strong> Paine connects political legitimacy with public welfare. He criticizes aristocratic privilege and proposes reforms such as public support for education and relief for poverty. The book is an intervention in arguments about monarchy, revolution, and democracy, not a neutral history. Learn its central contrast: inherited rank versus rights and consent."
    ],
    "7|A Vindication of the Rights of Woman": [
      "<strong>Argument:</strong> Wollstonecraft challenges writers who claim women are naturally less rational. She argues that girls are trained to seek beauty, dependence, and approval, then blamed for lacking the intellectual strength they were denied the chance to develop. If reason and virtue matter, women need serious education and the freedom to use their minds.",
      "<strong>Conclusion:</strong> Wollstonecraft calls for a more equal society in which women can become independent citizens and partners rather than ornamental dependents. She also criticizes men’s education and social behavior; the problem is not simply women’s individual choices but an unjust system of training and expectations. The book argues for rational education and mutual respect."
    ],
    "8|Don Quixote": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Alonso Quixano, a country gentleman, reads so many tales of chivalry that he loses his ordinary sense of the world. He renames himself Don Quixote, recruits his practical neighbor Sancho Panza as squire, and rides out to defend the helpless. Windmills become giants, inns become castles, and ordinary travelers become characters in heroic quests. Sancho often sees what is actually there, but is also drawn into his master’s hopes and promises of reward.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> In the second part, Don Quixote’s adventures are famous, and other characters use that fame to trick him. The Duke and Duchess stage cruel performances; Sancho briefly governs an island and proves wiser than expected. Eventually a disguised neighbor defeats Don Quixote and sends him home. Alonso regains his reason, renounces knight-errantry, and dies. The ending turns comic misreadings into a question about imagination, dignity, and how others exploit a dreamer. <a href='https://assets.cambridge.org/97805213/13452/excerpt/9780521313452_excerpt.pdf' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Cambridge University Press overview ↗</a>"
    ],
    "8|Jane Eyre": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Jane is an orphan raised by an unkind aunt and sent to Lowood School, where hunger, illness, and strict discipline shape her childhood. She becomes a teacher, then a governess at Thornfield Hall. Jane falls in love with the house’s master, Edward Rochester, but on their wedding day learns that he already has a wife, Bertha Mason, hidden in the house. Jane refuses to become his secret partner and leaves with almost nothing.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> The Rivers siblings take Jane in, and she discovers they are her cousins; an inheritance gives her financial independence. She hears Rochester calling to her and returns to find Thornfield burned. Bertha died in the fire, and Rochester was injured and has lost his sight. Jane chooses to marry him when she can do so freely and on more equal terms. The resolution depends on Jane’s moral independence, not simply on winning a wealthy husband."
    ],
    "8|Pride and Prejudice": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> The Bennet family faces pressure to secure marriages because their estate will pass to a male relative. When wealthy Charles Bingley rents a nearby house, Jane Bennet and he fall in love, while Elizabeth meets his friend Mr Darcy and judges him proud. Darcy’s first proposal confirms her worst impression; she rejects him and accuses him of separating Jane and Bingley and mistreating the officer George Wickham.",
      "<strong>Change and ending:</strong> Darcy’s letter reveals that Wickham has lied and that Darcy believed Jane did not return Bingley’s affection. Elizabeth recognizes that she misread people, while Darcy tries to become less arrogant. Lydia runs away with Wickham, threatening the family’s reputation; Darcy quietly arranges their marriage. Bingley returns to Jane, and Darcy proposes again. Elizabeth accepts after both have learned to revise their pride and prejudice."
    ],
    "8|The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes": [
      "<strong>What the collection contains:</strong> This is a series of cases narrated mostly by Dr Watson, not one continuous mystery. Clients bring Holmes strange clues: a secret society that exists to employ a man, a missing treasure, a cryptic message, or a threat hidden in an ordinary household. Watson often records the facts without knowing which detail matters. Holmes notices patterns in clothing, timing, marks, or behavior and turns them into an explanation.",
      "<strong>How to read it:</strong> Each story builds suspense by limiting what the narrator and reader know. Holmes’s deductions can be clever but also depend on assumptions, and Watson’s admiration shapes the portrait of the detective. The pleasure is watching an ordinary clue change meaning once the surrounding facts are known. Different editions select different stories, so check which cases your course assigns."
    ],
    "9|Oliver Twist": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Oliver grows up in a parish workhouse where the poor are treated as problems to manage. After asking for more food, he is apprenticed to an undertaker and escapes to London. There he is taken in by Fagin, who trains homeless boys to steal. Oliver is innocent of their crimes, but the brutal Bill Sikes and the gang pull him into danger. A wealthy gentleman, Mr Brownlow, briefly gives him a safe home before the gang recaptures him.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Nancy risks her life to tell Rose Maylie and Brownlow that Oliver is in danger and reveal what she knows about his birth. Sikes murders Nancy and later dies while fleeing; the criminal group collapses. Brownlow discovers Oliver’s family history, and Oliver is adopted into a safer household. Dickens exposes the cruelty of workhouses and child exploitation, though the rescue ending also relies on the Victorian idea that hidden good birth can secure a child’s future."
    ],
    "9|Hard Times": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> In the factory town of Coketown, schoolmaster Thomas Gradgrind believes children should learn facts and suppress imagination. His daughter Louisa is trained to hide her feelings; his son Tom grows selfish; Sissy Jupe, raised among circus performers, brings kindness into the household. Louisa marries the much older industrialist Josiah Bounderby though she does not love him. Her brother Tom works for Bounderby and falls into debt and gambling.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Tom robs a bank and lets an innocent factory worker, Stephen Blackpool, be suspected. Louisa nearly leaves her husband for James Harthouse, but her father sees what his education has done and takes her home. Sissy helps expose Tom, who escapes abroad; Bounderby’s story of rising from poverty is revealed as a lie. Louisa remains unmarried, while Gradgrind begins to value feeling and imagination. Dickens attacks a system that reduces people to statistics and labor."
    ],
    "9|Dracula": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Jonathan Harker travels to Transylvania to complete a property transaction and discovers that his host, Count Dracula, is a vampire planning to move to England. Harker is imprisoned in the castle; Dracula reaches England with boxes of native earth. Mina Murray and her friend Lucy Westenra become involved as Dracula preys on Lucy. Doctor Van Helsing recognizes the danger, but Lucy dies and rises as a vampire before the group can stop her.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Mina and the men compare diaries and letters to understand Dracula’s movements. Dracula attacks Mina, binding her to him, so the group pursues him back toward Transylvania. They catch his transport before it reaches safety and destroy him; Quincey Morris is mortally wounded, but Mina is freed from the vampire’s influence. The documentary form makes the characters assemble evidence like investigators, while the monster brings anxieties about invasion, sexuality, science, and social change."
    ],
    "9|Strange Case of Dr Jekyll and Mr Hyde": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Lawyer Gabriel Utterson hears that a violent man named Edward Hyde has been given access to the respectable Dr Henry Jekyll’s home and fortune. Hyde tramples a child, then murders Sir Danvers Carew. Jekyll first promises to cut Hyde off, but later shuts himself away. Utterson and Jekyll’s friend Dr Lanyon each uncover pieces of the secret; Lanyon is so horrified by what he sees that he falls ill and dies.",
      "<strong>Reveal and ending:</strong> Documents left by Lanyon and Jekyll explain the mystery. Jekyll developed a potion intended to separate his respectable self from forbidden impulses. The transformation produces Hyde, who becomes increasingly violent and eventually appears without the potion. Jekyll cannot control when he changes and fears arrest. His final confession ends with Hyde’s body dead in Jekyll’s room. Stevenson turns a London mystery into a Gothic question about divided identity, repression, and the danger of imagining evil can be safely separated from oneself."
    ],
    "9|“The White Man’s Burden”": [
      "<strong>What the poem says:</strong> Kipling addresses the United States as it expands its colonial power after the Spanish-American War. The speaker describes empire as a difficult duty: the imperial nation claims to educate and govern peoples it portrays as childlike or incapable of self-rule. A repeated command frames domination as noble sacrifice rather than as a pursuit of territory and power.",
      "<strong>How to read it critically:</strong> The poem is an example of paternalistic imperial ideology, not a neutral account of colonized people. Its language gives the colonizer moral authority while denying colonized societies equal agency. Ask who is allowed to speak, who is described as needing help, and whose interests the supposed burden serves. The title has since been used critically to challenge the idea that conquest is benevolent."
    ],
    "9|Hopes and Impediments": [
      "<strong>What the book contains:</strong> Achebe’s essays discuss African writing, colonial literature, culture, and the right of African writers to tell their own histories. In “An Image of Africa,” he criticizes Joseph Conrad’s Heart of Darkness for making Africa a backdrop for European fears while denying African characters full humanity. Other essays reflect on language, oral traditions, readership, and the responsibilities of the writer.",
      "<strong>Why it matters:</strong> This is a collection of arguments, not a single story. Achebe asks readers to examine who gets to represent whom and how literary prestige can make stereotypes look like truth. Read it alongside, rather than as a replacement for, the works it critiques; comparison helps distinguish Conrad’s artistic choices from Achebe’s later challenge to them."
    ],
    "9|Wuthering Heights": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> The tenant Lockwood visits Wuthering Heights and hears housekeeper Nelly Dean recount two generations of family history. As a child, Heathcliff is brought into the Earnshaw family and becomes inseparable from Catherine. Catherine loves him but marries Edgar Linton because of class and security. Heathcliff overhears part of her confession, leaves, then returns wealthy and determined to punish both families. Catherine dies after giving birth to a daughter, Cathy.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Heathcliff’s revenge reaches the next generation: he controls Cathy and forces her to marry his sickly son Linton, and keeps Hareton uneducated and dependent. After Linton dies, young Cathy and Hareton gradually become friends and fall in love, breaking the pattern of cruelty. Heathcliff, increasingly consumed by memories of Catherine, stops pursuing revenge and dies. Lockwood later sees their graves and hears of the young couple’s plans. The frame narrative and Gothic atmosphere make this about obsession and inheritance, not a simple romance. <a href='https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/768' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Project Gutenberg text ↗</a>"
    ],
    "10|Sons and Lovers": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Paul Morel grows up in a mining family where his mother Gertrude and father Walter argue over money, class, and their failed marriage. Gertrude turns much of her affection toward her sons. Paul is talented at drawing and finds work, but the death of his older brother William deepens his mother’s dependence on him. He grows close to Miriam Leivers, whose religious seriousness and love of nature attract him, yet he resists marrying her.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Paul later has an affair with Clara Dawes, an older married woman, but neither relationship frees him from the emotional bond with his mother. When Gertrude becomes terminally ill, Paul gives her morphine to end her suffering. After her death, he feels pulled toward death himself, then turns toward the city and life. The novel’s ending is unresolved: he must decide who he is apart from his mother and whether he can form an adult attachment."
    ],
    "10|“Tickets, Please!”": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> During World War I, women operate a tram line in the English Midlands while men are away. The conductresses work in harsh weather, manage unruly passengers, and develop a lively camaraderie. Inspector John Joseph Raynor oversees the route and flirts with several women. Annie becomes attached to him and expects the attention to mean more than it does; when Raynor turns to another woman, she and the other conductresses arrange to confront him.",
      "<strong>Ending and edition note:</strong> Their confrontation turns physical, exposing both the women’s collective anger and the violence Raynor has helped set in motion. The women force him to choose, and he names Annie, but the choice does not restore trust or make her happy. The story closes with a bruised, uncomfortable feeling rather than a clean victory. Lawrence substantially revised the story before its 1922 collection, especially the ending, so the version assigned in class matters. <a href='https://archive.org/details/englandmyengland00lawrrich' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Collection text ↗</a> · <a href='https://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/id/eprint/130206/1/WRAP_Theses_Concepcion_1993.pdf' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Warwick research on the revision ↗</a>"
    ],
    "10|Ulysses": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> James Joyce’s novel follows one ordinary day—June 16, 1904—in Dublin. Stephen Dedalus, a young teacher and aspiring writer, spends the morning at a Martello tower and later wanders through the city, carrying grief over his mother’s death and uncertainty about his future. Leopold Bloom, a Jewish advertising canvasser, leaves home to run errands, attend a funeral, place an advertisement, eat lunch, and think about his wife Molly, their dead infant son Rudy, and their daughter Milly.",
      "<strong>Turning point and ending:</strong> Bloom knows that Molly is expecting a visit from concert manager Blazes Boylan, and that Boylan will likely become her lover that afternoon; he still moves through the day, observing strangers and helping where he can. Stephen and Bloom cross paths, meet in a maternity hospital, and later share an uneasy night in the red-light district. Bloom brings Stephen home, offers him cocoa, and imagines him as a surrogate son, but Stephen leaves. Bloom returns to bed beside Molly. In the final episode, Molly’s restless memories turn toward her marriage proposal on Howth and end with an emphatic yes. The novel loosely echoes Homer’s Odyssey but turns errands, thoughts, grief, and city life into epic material. <a href='https://press.jhu.edu/books/title/12755/guide-james-joyces-ulysses' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Johns Hopkins University Press episode-guide reference ↗</a> · <a href='https://www.ulyssesguide.com/18-penelope' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Episode guide to Molly’s closing monologue ↗</a> · <a href='https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/4300' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Read the novel at Project Gutenberg ↗</a>"
    ],
    "10|Mrs Dalloway": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Clarissa Dalloway walks through London to buy flowers for a party she will host that evening. Ordinary streets stir memories of her youth at Bourton, especially her friendship with Sally Seton and her former suitor Peter Walsh. At the same time, Septimus Warren Smith, a veteran traumatized by war, walks through the city with his Italian wife, Lucrezia. Septimus hears voices and feels unable to return to ordinary life; doctors insist on controlling him instead of understanding him.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Clarissa’s party brings together political guests and old acquaintances. Dr Bradshaw arrives late and mentions that Septimus has died by suicide after jumping from a window rather than be taken away for treatment. Clarissa briefly withdraws to consider his death and the pressure placed on him, then returns to her guests. The two characters never meet, but their parallel experiences join private memory, mortality, class, social performance, and society’s treatment of mental distress. The story lasts one day while moving repeatedly through the past."
    ],
    "10|The Waste Land": [
      "<strong>What happens:</strong> This five-part poem has no single continuous plot. Its voices shift among a speaker remembering a difficult season, a fortune-teller, city workers crossing London Bridge, a pub conversation, a drowned sailor, and figures drawn from myth and scripture. Fragments of music, different languages, and older literary works appear beside modern scenes. Tiresias, the blind prophet, offers one thread connecting some of the voices, but he does not turn the poem into a simple narrative.",
      "<strong>Ending and effect:</strong> The last section moves through images of collapse, thunder, and broken fragments before ending with words drawn from ancient Indian texts. Readers disagree about how much hope the ending offers. Eliot’s broken form makes the reader experience cultural dislocation instead of merely hearing it described. The poem is often read in relation to World War I, but its sources and themes are broader than one event."
    ],
    "10|Murder in the Cathedral": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Archbishop Thomas Becket returns to Canterbury after years of exile and knows that King Henry II’s conflict with the Church may lead to his death. A chorus of ordinary Canterbury women fears the danger approaching their community. Tempters visit Becket: they offer personal safety, political power, the king’s friendship, and finally the glory of choosing martyrdom. Becket must decide whether accepting death for fame would corrupt the sacrifice itself.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Becket preaches about the meaning of martyrdom, then four knights arrive and murder him in the cathedral. The chorus speaks of the spiritual and social cost of the violence. The knights then defend their actions to the audience in language that sounds like a public justification. The verse drama makes the murder both a historical event and a question about power, conscience, faith, and the temptation to turn even sacrifice into self-promotion."
    ],
    "10|Mrs Warren’s Profession": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Vivie Warren is an educated young woman who expects to earn her own living. She learns that her mother, Mrs Warren, has made money through brothels and is still a partner in the business. Mrs Warren explains that poverty and limited respectable jobs pushed her into prostitution, and that she built financial security rather than accept hunger. Vivie struggles to reconcile her mother’s affection with the continued exploitation behind the business.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Sir George Crofts, a wealthy business associate of Mrs Warren, tries to make Vivie accept a marriage that would bind her to his money and power. Vivie refuses. She also rejects her mother’s continuing business, even after learning the social conditions that shaped it, and chooses independent office work. Shaw asks the audience to examine the economic arrangements beneath Victorian sexual morality; Vivie’s independence is real but emotionally costly."
    ],
    "10|The Land of Heart’s Desire": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> On May Eve, the newly married Mary Bruin is surrounded by her husband Shawn, his family, and domestic duties. An otherworldly fairy child appears and offers Mary escape into a world of beauty, freedom, and endless youth. Mary is drawn to the offer, while the others try to hold her to ordinary family life. The play’s Irish folkloric setting makes the fairy world both alluring and dangerous.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Mary follows the child’s call and collapses; her family cannot bring her back to ordinary consciousness, and she dies. The ending leaves open whether she has been taken into a supernatural realm or has been overcome by a force her family cannot understand. Yeats stages a conflict between human attachment and escape from domestic limits, while also asking what is lost when a person’s longing is dismissed as childish."
    ],
    "10|The Man of Property": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Soames Forsyte is a wealthy solicitor who regards people and beautiful objects alike as things to possess. He marries Irene, who does not love him, and commissions architect Philip Bosinney to build a country house. Bosinney falls in love with Irene, and she begins to imagine a life beyond Soames’s control. Soames sues Bosinney for exceeding the building budget, tightening pressure on the young architect and making the household conflict public.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Bosinney dies after being struck by a cab, and Irene leaves Soames. Soames can legally claim his wife, but cannot make her love him or willingly remain. The Forsyte family treats the scandal as a threat to respectability and property. Galsworthy uses the family saga to examine class, marriage, possession, and the limits of a society that measures people by what they own."
    ],
    "10|Labyrinths": [
      "<strong>What the collection contains:</strong> Borges’s book gathers short fiction and essays; it has no single plot. In “The Library of Babel,” a narrator imagines an immense library containing every possible combination of letters, so the search for one meaningful book becomes both a source of hope and despair. In “The Garden of Forking Paths,” a spy’s attempt to send a message intersects with a scholar’s explanation of an ancestor who created a novel-like labyrinth of branching time.",
      "<strong>How to read it:</strong> Other pieces use mirrors, doubles, invented books, and impossible puzzles to unsettle the line between reality and fiction. The stories are short, but their narrators often make confident claims that the reader must question. Borges turns ideas—time, infinity, authorship, identity—into narrative traps, then invites readers to decide whether the puzzle has been solved."
    ],
    "10|If on a winter’s night a traveler": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> The novel addresses its reader as “you,” who begins to read a book called If on a winter’s night a traveler, only to discover that the printed copies are faulty. At a bookstore, you meet another reader, Ludmilla. You and she repeatedly try to continue, but each replacement book turns out to be a different novel and stops at a suspenseful moment. The book alternates these abandoned story openings with the continuing adventures of you and Ludmilla.",
      "<strong>Ending and effect:</strong> The search brings the Reader into contact with translators, publishers, an author, and people who want to control or counterfeit books. The novel’s structure makes interruption part of its plot: you keep wanting a complete story, while Calvino keeps showing how stories begin, circulate, and get read. The frame finally turns toward an ordinary shared life and reading in bed, without resolving every unfinished narrative."
    ],
    "10|The Postmodern Condition": [
      "<strong>Argument:</strong> Lyotard’s book is a philosophical report on knowledge in technologically advanced societies, not a novel. It asks how scientific and cultural knowledge earns legitimacy when information is increasingly stored and transmitted by machines. Lyotard argues that confidence in large stories that promise universal progress, emancipation, or a single explanation of history has weakened.",
      "<strong>What to remember:</strong> He describes knowledge as moving among different language games, each with its own rules for what counts as a valid statement. The book’s famous idea of incredulity toward metanarratives does not mean that no one can know anything. It means that broad claims to universal authority should be examined, especially when they hide who benefits from them."
    ],
    "10|Black Mirror: Bandersnatch": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Stefan Butler is a young programmer in 1984 who adapts a branching fantasy book into a computer game. His work is shaped by grief over his mother’s death, pressure from his father, and conversations with therapist Dr Haynes. The viewer makes choices for him—first small ones such as breakfast cereal, then decisions that affect his work, relationships, and safety. Some branches lead to different endings, while others make Stefan suspect someone is controlling him.",
      "<strong>Ending and effect:</strong> The story can end with Stefan’s game acclaimed, delayed, or destroyed, and with his fate changing according to the choices made. There is no single path that fully explains every branch. The interactive form makes the audience feel responsible for events, then asks how much control the viewer really has when the options and consequences were designed in advance."
    ],
    "11|The Scarlet Letter": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> In Puritan Boston, Hester Prynne is publicly punished for adultery and forced to wear a scarlet A on her clothing. She refuses to name the child’s father, Reverend Arthur Dimmesdale, who continues preaching while hiding his guilt. Hester’s estranged husband, Roger Chillingworth, arrives under another name and becomes Dimmesdale’s physician, using his access to torment the minister. Hester’s daughter Pearl grows up curious and defiant under the town’s judgment.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Hester meets Dimmesdale in the forest and they plan to leave for Europe with Pearl. At a public election-day ceremony, Dimmesdale instead confesses on the scaffold and dies. Chillingworth loses the purpose of his revenge and dies soon afterward; Pearl inherits his property and disappears from the town’s daily life. Hawthorne contrasts visible shame, private guilt, and public reputation without offering a simple moral verdict."
    ],
    "11|Nineteen Eighty-Four": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Winston Smith works for the Ministry of Truth, where he alters old documents so the Party’s current claims always appear correct. In a society of telescreens, informers, and Big Brother posters, even private thoughts can be treated as crimes. Winston begins a diary and falls in love with Julia. They rent a room they believe is hidden from surveillance, but the apparent refuge is a trap.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Party official O’Brien reveals that the Thought Police have watched Winston. In the Ministry of Love, Winston is tortured until he accepts the Party’s version of reality and betrays Julia. Room 101 confronts him with his worst fear. When he is released, he no longer resists or loves Julia; he loves Big Brother. Orwell’s ending shows the regime’s goal as the destruction of independent thought, not just obedience."
    ],
    "11|The Hunger Games": [
      "<strong>Story and conflict:</strong> Katniss Everdeen volunteers for her younger sister Prim and is sent from District 12 into the Capitol’s televised death match with Peeta Mellark. She survives through hunting skills, alliances, help from sponsors, and a bond with Rue, a young tribute whose death becomes a public act of mourning. Katniss’s care for Rue makes the Capitol’s spectacle look cruel rather than entertaining.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> The rules change to allow two victors from the same district, so Katniss and Peeta survive together. When the Capitol reverses the rule and demands one survivor, they threaten to eat poisonous berries instead. Officials name them co-winners, but their refusal exposes the government’s limits and turns Katniss into a symbol of resistance. The ending is a victory for survival and the beginning of political danger."
    ],
    "13|Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening": [
      "<strong>Scene:</strong> A traveler pauses with a horse beside woods filling with snow. He knows the owner lives in the village and will not see him stop. The horse shakes its harness bells as if asking why they have halted at a dark, quiet place with no farmhouse nearby. The woods attract the traveler with their beauty and stillness.",
      "<strong>Ending and effect:</strong> The traveler acknowledges the woods’ pull, then remembers promises and continues on. The repeated closing idea of miles still to travel lets the poem remain suspended between rest and responsibility. Its regular rhythm sounds almost like a lullaby, while the returning rhyme and the traveler’s obligations keep the quiet scene from becoming a simple escape. The poem has snow and a horse, not the apple imagery mentioned elsewhere in the lesson."
    ],
    "13|Wild Geese": [
      "<strong>Movement of the poem:</strong> Oliver’s speaker begins by addressing a listener who feels guilty, lonely, or unworthy of belonging. Instead of demanding perfect behavior, the poem points toward the body’s ordinary needs and the natural world: weather, landscape, and wild geese continue their lives beyond human self-judgment.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> The geese’s call becomes an invitation to recognize that a person belongs to the larger living world even while feeling imperfect. The poem moves from inward shame to outward attention and connection. Nature is not a reward for being good; it is already part of the world a reader can hear and inhabit. Its direct, welcoming voice is a major part of its effect."
    ],
    "13|Ode to a Nightingale": [
      "<strong>Movement of the poem:</strong> The speaker hears a nightingale singing and feels both pleasure and pain: the bird’s music is beautiful, but it makes human suffering and mortality more noticeable. He imagines escaping through wine, then through poetry, and mentally travels toward the bird’s song. The poem recalls figures such as Ruth and imagines the song reaching people across distant times.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> The speaker briefly welcomes death as a way to stop suffering, then recognizes that the bird’s apparent immortality belongs to the song, not to a single living bird. The music fades as the bird flies away. He is left asking whether the experience was real or a dream. The poem’s movement from desire for escape back to uncertain human life is as important as its beautiful description of the nightingale."
    ],
    "13|Sonnet 18": [
      "<strong>What the speaker does:</strong> The speaker begins by asking whether the beloved can be compared to a summer’s day, then lists reasons summer is unreliable: it can be too hot, too windy, or too brief. The beloved’s beauty is imagined as more constant than the season’s changing weather. The poem does not depend on a dramatic plot; its argument develops from comparison to a claim about time.",
      "<strong>Ending and form:</strong> The final couplet says that as long as people read the poem, the beloved’s beauty will continue to live in its lines. The poem thus presents writing as a defense against time, while also making its promise depend on future readers. It is a Shakespearean sonnet of fourteen lines, usually in iambic pentameter, with the rhyme pattern ABAB CDCD EFEF GG."
    ],
    "13|The Raven": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Late at night, a grieving speaker reads to distract himself from the death of Lenore. A tapping at the door leads to a raven entering his room and perching above the door. At first the speaker jokes about the bird and asks its name. The raven gives the same answer—“Nevermore”—to each question, but the speaker increasingly treats the repeated word as a judgment on his grief and future.",
      "<strong>Ending and effect:</strong> The speaker asks whether he will ever reunite with Lenore and whether the bird will leave. The answer never changes, yet his questions become more desperate. The raven remains while the speaker imagines that his soul is trapped beneath its shadow. Poe’s regular beat, repeated sounds, and refrain make the speaker’s movement from curiosity to obsession audible."
    ],
    "13|Still I Rise": [
      "<strong>Voice and movement:</strong> Angelou creates a speaker who addresses forces that try to humiliate, silence, or distort her. The speaker imagines how an opponent might resent her confidence, then answers with pride, humor, and images of abundance. Refrains return like a response to repeated attacks rather than a single confrontation.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> The speaker connects personal resilience with the history of ancestors who endured slavery and oppression. The rising image grows from an individual refusal to be diminished into a collective claim to survival and dignity. The poem does not deny violence; it refuses to let an oppressor define the speaker’s worth or future."
    ],
    "13|My Life had stood": [
      "<strong>What happens:</strong> Dickinson’s speaker imagines a loaded gun standing unused in a corner until an owner recognizes it, carries it away, and gives it a purpose. Together they hunt, watch over landscapes, and share an intense bond. The gun’s voice makes it seem powerful and active, but its activity depends on the person holding it.",
      "<strong>Ending and interpretation:</strong> The final image imagines the owner dying while the gun continues to live, yet the gun cannot act without the owner. The poem can suggest agency, dependence, protection, violence, devotion, or a speaker’s relation to a powerful patron; no single reading settles it. The proper title is commonly identified by its opening words, “My Life had stood — a Loaded Gun,” and the author is Emily Dickinson."
    ],
    "13|Harlem": [
      "<strong>Questions and images:</strong> Hughes asks what happens to a dream that has been postponed. The poem does not answer immediately; it tests a series of physical images, asking whether the dream dries, festers, smells, or becomes heavy. Each image makes delay feel more bodily and difficult to ignore.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> The final possibility is that the postponed dream may explode. That brief ending changes the poem’s quiet questions into a warning about pressure and consequences. Read “dream” in the context of blocked opportunity and racial injustice, not only as an individual wish. The poem offers no single explanation for what happens next; its force is in making the reader consider the cost of delay."
    ],
    "13|Twenty Love Poems and a Song of Despair": [
      "<strong>What the book contains:</strong> This is a collection, not one long poem with a single plot. Across the love poems, a speaker describes desire, closeness, remembered intimacy, and the changing body through images of night, weather, landscape, and touch. Some poems celebrate an unnamed beloved; others already sense distance or separation.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> The final “Song of Despair” turns toward absence and the pain of losing a relationship. The collection therefore moves through desire and memory rather than toward a conventional resolution. Its natural imagery is powerful but not merely decorative: the speaker uses sea, darkness, and seasons to make emotional states feel physical. The exact relationship between the poems and Neruda’s life should not be assumed from the lyric voice alone."
    ],
    "14|Oedipus Rex": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> A plague devastates Thebes. King Oedipus promises to find who murdered the former king, Laius, because the city has been told that the killer must be found. The prophet Tiresias resists the investigation, then says Oedipus himself is responsible. Oedipus suspects a political plot. His wife Jocasta tries to calm him by describing the prophecy she and Laius once tried to defeat.",
      "<strong>Recognition and ending:</strong> A messenger and an old shepherd reveal that Oedipus was taken from his birth parents as a baby. On the road years earlier, he unknowingly killed Laius, his father, and later married Jocasta, his mother. Jocasta kills herself; Oedipus blinds himself and asks to be exiled. An investigation meant to cleanse the city becomes a devastating discovery about identity, knowledge, and responsibility."
    ],
    "14|Macbeth": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Returning from battle, Macbeth and Banquo meet three witches who predict that Macbeth will become king. Lady Macbeth urges him to seize the crown, and he murders King Duncan while Duncan is a guest in his castle. Macbeth takes the throne but becomes increasingly afraid of losing it. He arranges Banquo’s murder, while Banquo’s son Fleance escapes. Macbeth orders the killing of Macduff’s family when he sees the nobleman as a threat.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Lady Macbeth is consumed by guilt and dies. Malcolm’s forces advance using branches cut from Birnam Wood, fulfilling another prophecy. Macbeth believes no one born of a woman can kill him, until Macduff reveals he was delivered by Caesarean birth. Macduff kills Macbeth, and Malcolm becomes king. The play shows ambition becoming tyranny through choices; prophecy shapes Macbeth’s imagination, but it does not make him innocent of his actions. <a href='https://www.rsc.org.uk/macbeth/' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Royal Shakespeare Company synopsis ↗</a>"
    ],
    "14|Death of a Salesman": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Willy Loman returns home exhausted after a driving trip and can no longer sustain his career as a traveling salesman. He clings to the belief that being popular and well-liked guarantees success. His wife Linda tries to protect him, while sons Biff and Happy struggle with his expectations. Memories show that Biff once admired Willy, then discovered Willy’s affair in Boston and lost faith in him. Biff has since drifted through jobs and failed attempts to become successful.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Biff tells Willy the family must stop pretending, but Willy interprets the confrontation as proof that Biff loves him and might prosper from an insurance payment. He drives away and dies by suicide. At the funeral, Linda says the house is finally paid off, but no one comes to celebrate. Biff rejects his father’s dream of popularity; Happy vows to continue it. Miller makes an ordinary salesman’s suffering tragic while questioning the American dream Willy believes in."
    ],
    "14|A Doll's House": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Nora Helmer has secretly borrowed money and forged her father’s signature so she could pay for a trip that saved her husband Torvald’s health. She has hidden the debt and made small economies to repay it. When the bank employee Krogstad threatens to expose her, Nora expects Torvald to protect her. Instead, when he learns the truth, he panics about his reputation and tells her she is unfit to raise their children.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> A letter removes the immediate threat, and Torvald wants to return to the marriage as if nothing happened. Nora realizes that he values his social standing over understanding her sacrifice, and that both her father and husband have treated her as a doll to please them. She leaves the house to learn how to live independently. Ibsen’s ending asks whether a marriage can be equal when one partner has never been allowed a full adult voice."
    ],
    "14|Waiting for Godot": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Vladimir and Estragon wait beside a tree for a man named Godot, though they cannot agree exactly when he promised to come. They consider leaving, quarrel, reconcile, and pass time with jokes, memories, and small routines. Pozzo and his servant Lucky arrive; Pozzo gives commands while Lucky carries luggage and performs when ordered. A boy delivers the message that Godot will not come today but will come tomorrow.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> The next day repeats the pattern, but Pozzo is now blind and Lucky cannot speak. The tree has a few leaves, while the men’s memories remain uncertain. The boy again says Godot will not come until tomorrow. Vladimir and Estragon say they will leave, but do not move. Godot never appears, and the play never identifies him conclusively. Repetition and unresolved waiting make the audience experience uncertainty rather than solve a conventional plot."
    ],
    "14|The Cherry Orchard": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Lyubov Ranevskaya returns from Paris to her family estate, which is deeply in debt and may be sold. The merchant Lopakhin proposes dividing the land into plots for summer cottages, a practical plan that would preserve the family’s money but require cutting down the cherished cherry orchard. Ranevskaya and her brother Gaev cannot accept the proposal and keep hoping for another rescue. Their daughter Anya and adopted daughter Varya face different futures.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Lopakhin buys the estate at auction and announces that he will cut down the orchard. The family leaves, scattered to new arrangements and uncertain work. In the confusion, the elderly servant Firs is forgotten and locked in the empty house as the sound of axes begins. Chekhov combines comedy with loss: the family mourns a beloved place but has failed to act, while a new social class takes ownership."
    ],
    "14|Mother Courage and Her Children": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Anna Fierling, known as Mother Courage, pulls a supply wagon behind armies during the Thirty Years’ War and tries to support herself by selling goods to soldiers. She travels with her children Eilif, Swiss Cheese, and the mute Kattrin. War gives her customers and a livelihood, but each child is drawn into danger. Eilif is praised for a violent act during wartime, then later executed for a similar act when the rules change. Swiss Cheese is captured and killed after Courage delays too long bargaining for his release.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Kattrin hears that a town is about to be attacked and climbs onto a roof to beat a drum, warning its people; soldiers shoot her. Mother Courage buries her and then hitches herself to the wagon to follow the army again. She survives, but the wagon and the need to keep trading prevent her from escaping the system that has taken her children. Brecht asks how war turns survival and profit into part of destruction."
    ],
    "14|A Raisin in the Sun": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> The Younger family—Walter and Ruth, their son Travis, Walter’s sister Beneatha, and their mother Lena—wait for an insurance payment after the death of Lena’s husband. Each person imagines a different future for the money: Walter wants to invest in a liquor store, Beneatha hopes to pay for medical school, and Lena wants a home with room and sunlight. She puts a down payment on a house in the white neighborhood of Clybourne Park.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Walter gives money to his business partner Willy Harris, who disappears with it, including the portion intended for Beneatha’s education. A white representative, Mr Lindner, offers to buy the family out so they will not move into the neighborhood. Walter briefly considers accepting, then rejects the offer and chooses to move. The family leaves with the future uncertain. Hansberry ties dreams of home and self-respect to racial discrimination, housing segregation, and family conflict."
    ],
    "14|Theatre of the Oppressed": [
      "<strong>What the book proposes:</strong> Boal’s work describes methods for making theatre a place to analyze power, not only watch a finished story. In Forum Theatre, actors present a scene of oppression that ends badly. Audience members can stop the action, replace a character, and try a different choice. Boal calls these participants “spect-actors” because they shift between watching and acting.",
      "<strong>How to picture its purpose:</strong> The rehearsal does not promise that a real social problem can be solved by one clever performance. It lets a group test strategies, notice obstacles, and discuss who has power before trying to act in life. The work is theatre theory and practice rather than one plot-based play. It turns the audience’s response into part of the form."
    ],
    "15|The Analects": [
      "<strong>What the book contains:</strong> The Analects is a collection of sayings and conversations associated with Confucius and preserved by followers; it is not a continuous story. Confucius answers students’ questions about learning, government, ritual, family, and how to become a morally cultivated person. Some passages model a teacher correcting a student, while others show an ideal ruler leading by example instead of fear.",
      "<strong>Key ideas:</strong> Ren means humaneness or care; li is ritual propriety and appropriate conduct; xiao is family responsibility or filial respect; junzi is an exemplary person formed through ethical practice. The passages do not reduce morality to blind obedience: respectful correction can be part of responsible relationships. Read the book as a set of linked ethical discussions, not as a single doctrine presented in one uninterrupted argument."
    ],
    "15|Bhagavad Gita": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> On the battlefield of Kurukshetra, the warrior Arjuna asks his charioteer Krishna to place the chariot between the two armies. He sees relatives, teachers, and friends on both sides and loses the will to fight. Krishna answers his crisis through a long dialogue about the self, duty, action, knowledge, devotion, and the nature of reality. At one point Krishna reveals a cosmic form that overwhelms Arjuna.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> Krishna teaches Arjuna to fulfill his dharma and act without attachment to personal reward, while recognizing the spiritual self beyond the changing body. The teaching does not make the battle emotionally simple; Arjuna still has to choose how to act amid painful obligations. By the end, he takes up his bow. The Gita is a philosophical and religious dialogue embedded in the Mahabharata, not a standalone war story."
    ],
    "15|Things Fall Apart": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Okonkwo has earned status in Umuofia through wrestling, farming, and titles. He is driven by fear of resembling his gentle father Unoka, and that fear makes him harsh toward his family. The boy Ikemefuna lives with Okonkwo’s household and becomes close to Nwoye, Okonkwo’s son. When the Oracle orders Ikemefuna’s death, Okonkwo takes part despite being warned not to. Later, an accidental killing forces Okonkwo into seven years of exile in his mother’s village.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Missionaries and colonial officials arrive while Okonkwo is away. Nwoye joins the church, and new courts and institutions alter village life. On returning, Okonkwo wants to fight the colonizers, but his community is divided. He kills a colonial messenger, realizes the clan will not rise with him, and takes his own life. The District Commissioner reduces Okonkwo’s life to a possible paragraph in a colonial book. Achebe’s ending exposes how an outsider’s account can erase the complexity of Igbo society."
    ],
    "15|Heart of Darkness": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> In a frame narrative, sailor Marlow tells companions how he traveled upriver in the Congo to retrieve ivory trader Kurtz for a European company. Along the way, he sees exploitation, forced labor, and cruelty hidden beneath claims of “civilizing” work. Kurtz is famous as an eloquent agent, but Marlow finds him gravely ill and wielding near-total power over a local community. Kurtz’s report for a humanitarian society ends with a violent postscript.",
      "<strong>Ending and critical lens:</strong> Kurtz dies after uttering “The horror!” Marlow returns to Europe and visits Kurtz’s Intended, who asks for his last words. Marlow tells her they were her name, withholding the truth. The story indicts imperial greed, but Achebe and other critics argue that Conrad also gives African characters too little voice and humanity. Reading both points together avoids treating criticism of empire as proof that every part of the novel escapes colonial assumptions."
    ],
    "15|Noli Me Tangere": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Crisostomo Ibarra returns to the Philippines after studying in Europe and hopes to marry Maria Clara and build a school. He is welcomed into a society shaped by colonial officials and friars, especially Padre Damaso, who humiliates him. Ibarra’s school project is obstructed, and Elias—a boatman who has experienced persecution—warns him that reform will not be easy. Maria Clara’s family history is tied to Damaso, though she and Ibarra do not know the full story at first.",
      "<strong>Ending:</strong> Ibarra is falsely implicated in an uprising and pursued by authorities. Elias helps him escape and is mortally wounded; before dying, he asks the boy Basilio to build a fire. Basilio finds his mother Sisa, who has been driven mad by the loss of her sons, and she dies. Maria Clara enters a convent after being separated from Ibarra. Rizal uses private tragedies to show abuses in institutions and the suffering of ordinary Filipinos. <a href='https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/6737' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Project Gutenberg English text ↗</a>"
    ],
    "15|The Miseducation of the Filipino": [
      "<strong>Argument:</strong> Constantino argues that colonial education trained Filipinos to interpret their own society through American interests and assumptions. Schools could teach literacy and provide opportunity while also making colonial rule seem natural, elevating foreign culture, and detaching learning from local needs. Education, in this account, is never just a neutral transfer of facts; it shapes identity and political imagination.",
      "<strong>Conclusion and caution:</strong> The essay calls for education that begins from Filipino history and serves Filipino communities, instead of treating the colonial model as universal. It does not mean that every school subject or foreign influence is inherently harmful. Ask who designed the curriculum, whose knowledge is treated as authoritative, and whether schooling prepares students to understand and improve their own society."
    ],
    "4|Manu and the Great Flood": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> In one version from the Shatapatha Brahmana, Manu finds a tiny fish and protects it in a jar. As it grows, he transfers it to larger waters. The fish warns him that a flood will destroy living beings and tells him to build a boat. When the waters rise, Manu ties the boat to the fish’s horn; the fish leads him to safety on a mountain.",
      "<strong>After the flood:</strong> Manu survives alone and performs a ritual offering. From it, a woman named Ida appears, and together they become ancestors of humanity in this tradition. Later Hindu tellings identify the fish with Vishnu’s Matsya avatar and add other details. Keep the versions distinct: these are related retellings, not one fixed story. <a href='https://msrvvp.ac.in/ved-vidya/30_Vedavidya.pdf' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Research on Manu and Hindu flood traditions ↗</a>"
    ],
    "4|Heliopolitan Creation Myth (the Ennead)": [
      "<strong>Creation sequence:</strong> In the Heliopolitan account, the world begins as Nun, the boundless primeval water. Atum brings himself into being and creates Shu, associated with air, and Tefnut, associated with moisture. Their descendants are Geb, the earth, and Nut, the sky. Geb and Nut become parents of Osiris, Isis, Seth, and Nephthys, completing the group often called the Ennead—the nine deities of this tradition.",
      "<strong>What to remember:</strong> The story gives cosmic forces family relationships and explains how the ordered world and divine generations emerge from the original waters. It is one major Egyptian creation account among several; Egyptian religious traditions varied by place and period. <a href='https://knowledge.uchicago.edu/record/4829/files/Johansen_uchicago_0330D_16525.pdf' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>University of Chicago research on Egyptian creation traditions ↗</a>"
    ],
    "16|The Lady with the Dog": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Dmitri Gurov, a Moscow banker who is dissatisfied with his marriage and treats women as brief distractions, meets Anna Sergeyevna while both are vacationing in Yalta. She is known as the lady with the little dog and is also unhappily married. They begin an affair, but Anna feels guilty and returns to her husband. Gurov expects to forget her; instead, he keeps remembering her and realizes that he has fallen deeply in love.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> Gurov eventually finds Anna in her provincial town, and they begin meeting secretly in Moscow. Their public lives remain separate from the relationship that now feels most real to them. The story ends with the two trying to work out how they might be together, just as their difficulty becomes clear. Chekhov gives no neat escape or moral verdict: the lovers have changed, but the future remains unresolved. <a href='https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/13415' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Chekhov stories at Project Gutenberg ↗</a>"
    ],
    "16|Hills Like White Elephants": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> At a small railway station in Spain, an American man and a young woman called Jig wait for a train. They order drinks and talk about the hills, the heat, and whether they should try a new drink. Gradually, their guarded conversation reveals that Jig is pregnant and the man wants her to have an operation, generally understood to be an abortion. He repeatedly calls it simple and insists that he wants only what she wants, while she asks whether their relationship can return to the way it was.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> The couple never clearly announces a decision. The train arrives; the man carries the bags to the other platform, then returns. Jig tells him she feels fine, but the brief exchange does not settle what she will do or what will happen to them. Hemingway’s omissions make readers infer the conflict from repetition, pauses, translations, and the contrasting landscape. <a href='https://anthology.lib.virginia.edu/work/Hemingway/hemingway-hills?view=pageImages' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>University of Virginia digital text and edition notes ↗</a>"
    ],
    "16|Shooting an Elephant": [
      "<strong>What happens:</strong> Orwell’s first-person narrator, a British police officer in colonial Burma, is called to deal with an elephant that has gone into a destructive state called must and has killed a local man. By the time he finds it, the elephant is quietly eating. The officer believes it is no longer a danger and does not want to kill a valuable working animal. A crowd has gathered behind him, however, and he feels they expect him to perform the role of a decisive colonial ruler.",
      "<strong>Ending and argument:</strong> Afraid of looking weak before the crowd, he shoots. The elephant takes a long time to die, and the officer later admits that he acted partly to avoid seeming ridiculous. The essay turns one disturbing event into an argument about imperialism: the colonized are oppressed, but the colonizer is also trapped into acting a part to preserve authority. Read the human narrator critically; the essay does not give the Burmese crowd a full voice. <a href='https://www.orwellfoundation.com/the-orwell-foundation/orwell/essays-and-other-works/shooting-an-elephant/' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>The Orwell Foundation: original essay ↗</a>"
    ],
    "16|Notes of a Native Son": [
      "<strong>The title essay:</strong> Baldwin remembers the final months of his father’s life, their difficult relationship, and the older man’s bitterness shaped by racism. Baldwin’s father dies on the day Baldwin’s youngest sibling is born and as a Harlem race riot breaks out. At the funeral, memories of family conflict mingle with the larger violence of segregation. Later, while working in New Jersey, Baldwin is refused service in a restaurant because he is Black; in a flash of rage, he throws a glass and escapes before the consequences catch up with him.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> Baldwin returns to his father’s life and sees how racial hatred can harden people and poison relationships. He refuses both to excuse his father’s cruelty and to treat hatred as a way forward. The book is a collection of essays, not a novel; the title piece combines memoir with an analysis of race, anger, history, and inheritance. <a href='https://search.worldcat.org/title/Notes-of-a-native-son/oclc/170299' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>WorldCat record and publisher description ↗</a>"
    ],
    "16|The Year of Magical Thinking": [
      "<strong>What happens:</strong> Joan Didion’s memoir begins after her husband, writer John Gregory Dunne, suddenly collapses and dies at home. At the hospital, their adult daughter Quintana is already critically ill. Didion moves through the practical aftermath—medical decisions, funeral arrangements, clothes, documents—while her mind repeatedly returns to the moment of his death and to memories of their long life together. She describes the irrational hope that if she keeps his shoes, he may still need them and return.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> The book moves backward and forward through memory rather than following a simple recovery story. Didion researches grief and illness while showing that understanding the facts does not make loss feel real all at once. “Magical thinking” names the bereaved mind’s attempts to bargain with reality. The memoir ends without presenting grief as solved: it records the gradual recognition that the life she shared with John has ended, even as memory continues. <a href='https://www.kirkusreviews.com/book-reviews/joan-didion/the-year-of-magical-thinking/' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Kirkus review and book overview ↗</a>"
    ],
    "16|I Know Why the Caged Bird Sings": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Maya Angelou tells of Marguerite—called Maya—and her older brother Bailey, who are sent as small children to live with their paternal grandmother, Momma, in Stamps, Arkansas. The segregated town and racist humiliations shape their childhood. Maya is sexually abused by her mother’s boyfriend; after she tells the truth and he is killed, she becomes nearly silent for years. A kind teacher, Mrs. Flowers, helps her rediscover the pleasure and power of spoken and written language. As the memoir follows Maya between Arkansas, California, and back, it shows her trying to understand family, race, safety, and belonging.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> In adolescence, Maya gradually becomes more confident, works as a streetcar conductor, and discovers her independence. At seventeen, she gives birth to a son and realizes she can love and care for him. The book closes on a beginning rather than a completed life: a young mother stepping into responsibility after a childhood marked by trauma. It is a memoir of racism and harm, but also of family, books, voice, and self-making. <a href='https://assets.penguinrandomhouse.com/book-resumes/AngelouMaya_IKnowWhyThe.pdf' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Penguin Random House reading guide ↗</a>"
    ],
    "16|The Woman Warrior": [
      "<strong>Shape of the book:</strong> Maxine Hong Kingston’s memoir blends her memories of growing up Chinese American in California with family stories, Chinese legends, and imagined possibilities. In “No Name Woman,” her mother tells her of an aunt whose pregnancy outside marriage brought public punishment and erasure; Kingston imagines several lives the aunt might have had. “White Tigers” retells the legend of the woman warrior Fa Mu Lan and lets Kingston imagine a woman’s power. “Shaman” recounts her mother Brave Orchid’s life and work in China before immigration.",
      "<strong>Ending and meaning:</strong> In “At the Western Palace,” Kingston’s aunt Moon Orchid arrives in America hoping to reunite with her husband, but he rejects her and she suffers a breakdown. The final chapter, “A Song for a Barbarian Reed Pipe,” returns to Kingston’s childhood struggle to speak and to her complicated relationship with her mother. By weaving legend, memory, and invention, the book shows how a daughter searches for a voice inside inherited stories—while leaving readers to notice that these stories are Kingston’s shaped recollections, not a single transparent record of Chinese culture. <a href='https://books.google.com/books?id=9jNyfxW4a6QC' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Publisher record and contents ↗</a>"
    ],
    "17|Frankenstein": [
      "<strong>Story:</strong> Arctic explorer Robert Walton writes letters about his voyage and the exhausted traveler he rescues from the ice: Victor Frankenstein. Victor tells how his childhood curiosity became an obsession with creating life. After secretly animating a being assembled from human remains, Victor is horrified by its appearance and abandons it. The unnamed Creature learns language and human history by secretly observing the De Lacey family, but when he approaches them, they reject him. He asks Victor to make a female companion so he will not be alone.",
      "<strong>Turning point and ending:</strong> Victor begins the second creation, then destroys it because he fears what the pair might do. The Creature retaliates by killing Victor’s friend Henry Clerval and, on Victor’s wedding night, his bride Elizabeth; Victor’s father dies soon after. Victor pursues the Creature across Europe and into the Arctic, where he dies aboard Walton’s ship. The Creature appears, grieves over Victor, and says he will end his own life before disappearing into the ice. Shelley’s frame narrative asks who bears responsibility when a creator refuses care, and how rejection can turn a longing for companionship into revenge. The Creature has no name; Frankenstein is the scientist. <a href='https://www.themorgan.org/collection/frankenstein/plot' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Morgan Library & Museum plot synopsis ↗</a> · <a href='https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/84' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Read the novel at Project Gutenberg ↗</a>"
    ],
    "17|A Theory of Adaptation": [
      "<strong>Main argument:</strong> Linda Hutcheon studies how stories move from one medium or context to another. She treats adaptation as a recognizable reworking of an earlier work and as a creative process in its own right—not simply a test of whether a film is faithful to a book. An adapter selects, reshapes, and interprets; a viewer or reader may experience the adaptation with or without knowing its source.",
      "<strong>How to use the idea:</strong> Hutcheon compares modes of engagement: telling a story through words, showing it through performance or images, and allowing audiences to interact with it. Each medium has different strengths and limits, so changes in point of view, pacing, scenes, or ending may be necessary or purposeful. When comparing a novel and film, ask what was transformed, what the new form can do, and how the adaptation’s historical audience and context affect its meaning. <a href='https://www.routledge.com/A-Theory-of-Adaptation/Hutcheon/p/book/9780203095010' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Routledge edition overview ↗</a>"
    ],
    "17|Literature and Film": [
      "<strong>What the book is:</strong> This is a scholarly collection edited by Robert Stam and Alessandra Raengo, not one continuous story. Its essays examine how literary works and films tell stories differently and how adaptations interpret their sources. The volume treats adaptation as a relationship among texts, media, viewers, and cultural settings, rather than a one-way transfer where the book is automatically superior.",
      "<strong>How to use it:</strong> Film can communicate through framing, editing, sound, acting, and images; literature can use narration, interior thought, and verbal style. A useful comparison identifies the source and film’s choices, then asks what those choices do to character, point of view, genre, and cultural meaning. The collection offers different critical approaches, so do not treat one essay’s method as the only correct way to compare books and films. <a href='https://www.wiley.com/en-gb/literature-and-film-a-guide-to-the-theory-and-practice-of-film-adaptation-p-9780631230557' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Wiley: collection description and editors ↗</a>"
    ],
    "17|The Work of Art in the Age of Mechanical Reproduction": [
      "<strong>Main argument:</strong> Walter Benjamin asks what happens to art when photography, film, and other technologies can make many copies of something that once had a unique physical presence. He calls the felt uniqueness and historical presence of an original its <em>aura</em>. Reproduction weakens the artwork’s dependence on a single place, ritual, or owner, while making it easier for large audiences to encounter and circulate images.",
      "<strong>Why it matters:</strong> Benjamin sees new possibilities and risks in mass media. A film can be edited, repeated, and shown to crowds, changing how people experience art and public life. Reproduction can broaden access, but it can also be used to shape politics. He contrasts fascism’s use of spectacle to aestheticize politics with the possibility of politicizing art. The essay is an argument about technology, culture, and power—not simply a claim that copies are inferior. <a href='https://modernistcommons.ca/islandora/object/yale%3A665' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Modernist Commons: essay context and scholarship ↗</a>"
    ],
    "17|Simulacra and Simulation": [
      "<strong>Main argument:</strong> Jean Baudrillard examines a society saturated with signs, images, models, and media. A <em>simulacrum</em> is a representation that no longer functions as a straightforward copy of a stable original. When models and signs shape what people experience as real, Baudrillard calls the condition <em>hyperreality</em>. The book is a set of philosophical essays, not a conventional plot or a practical manual for identifying fake images.",
      "<strong>How to read it:</strong> Baudrillard’s provocative examples ask whether representations merely describe reality or help organize it. Advertising, television, theme parks, and political spectacle can make the distinction between “the real” and its staged image difficult to maintain. His claims are deliberately sweeping and have been debated; use the key terms to analyze a specific example, and explain what the theory helps you see rather than repeating “everything is fake.” <a href='https://press.umich.edu/Books/S/Simulacra-and-Simulation' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>University of Michigan Press overview ↗</a>"
    ],
    "17|21 Lessons for the 21st Century": [
      "<strong>What the book covers:</strong> Yuval Noah Harari’s collection of 21 essays considers urgent political, technological, and personal questions. Its five broad sections address technological change; political challenges such as nationalism and community; hope and despair; truth and misinformation; and education, meaning, and resilience. Rather than telling one story, the chapters offer arguments and questions about a changing world.",
      "<strong>Central questions:</strong> Harari asks how people can make sense of rapid change, big data, artificial intelligence, biotechnology, political division, and competing public stories. He argues that people need to distinguish reliable information from narratives that manipulate them and to keep learning as institutions change. Treat the book as a set of claims to evaluate: identify the chapter’s evidence and assumptions, then consider what other perspectives might challenge it. <a href='https://www.penguinrandomhouse.co.za/sites/penguinbooks.co.za/files/Extract%20-%2021%20Lessons%20for%20the%2021st%20Century.pdf' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Publisher reading extract ↗</a>"
    ],
    "17|The Age of Surveillance Capitalism": [
      "<strong>Main argument:</strong> Shoshana Zuboff describes a business model in which companies collect traces of ordinary life—searches, clicks, locations, and other behavior—and use data beyond what is needed to provide a service. She calls the extra material <em>behavioral surplus</em>. Firms analyze it to create predictions about what people may do; those predictions can be sold or used to influence behavior. Zuboff calls this economic system surveillance capitalism.",
      "<strong>Why it matters:</strong> The book argues that the power to observe and predict people can become power to shape their choices, often without meaningful public understanding or control. Zuboff connects this asymmetry to privacy, autonomy, democracy, and the concentration of corporate power. Use the framework to ask what data a service collects, who benefits from it, what predictions are produced, and whether the design nudges behavior. <a href='https://shoshanazuboff.com/book/about/' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Shoshana Zuboff on the book’s argument ↗</a>"
    ],
  };

  const works = window.STUDY_DATA.works;
  const missing = [];
  for (const work of works) {
    const key = `${work.week}|${work.title}`;
    const paragraphs = expanded[key];
    if (!paragraphs) {
      missing.push(key);
      continue;
    }
    work.summary = paragraphs.map(paragraph => `<p>${paragraph}</p>`).join("");
    if (key === "13|My Life had stood") {
      work.title = "My Life had stood — a Loaded Gun";
      work.author = "Emily Dickinson";
    }
    if (key === "17|Literature and Film") work.author = "Edited by Robert Stam & Alessandra Raengo";
  }
  if (missing.length) throw new Error(`Missing expanded bookshelf summaries: ${missing.join(", ")}`);
  window.STUDY_DATA.works = works;
})();
